AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_single_sided_lp_AbstractSingleSidedLP_sol_getWithdrawRequestValue.txt
Trace:

  1. The claimed precondition is that a proportional exit returns exactly zero for one leg. The proportional exit is
  _unstakeAndExitPool(poolClaim, minAmounts, isSingleSided: false), which delegates into LP_LIB (CurveConvexLib) — code
  that is not shown in either the base file or the derived CurveConvex2Token slice. The claim's central mechanic
  therefore rests on unshown code.
  2. Taking the standard Curve remove_liquidity semantics anyway: amount_i = balance_i * lpBurned / totalSupply.
  Truncation to zero requires balance_i * lpBurned < totalSupply, i.e. balance_i < totalSupply / lpBurned. For a
  "maxed-out leveraged position" the vault's LP claim is a meaningful fraction of supply (and is explicitly bounded
  above, not below, by _checkPoolShare/MAX_POOL_SHARE), so balance_i would have to be driven down to single-digit or low
  wei. Under the StableSwap (and cryptoswap) invariant the D^(n+1)/(n^n·Πx) term diverges as any reserve approaches
  zero, so the input token amount required to push one reserve to ~wei-scale diverges without bound. A flash-swap cannot
  reach that state at any finite cost; the attacker can skew the pool heavily, but never to the point where the
  proportional share of a non-dust LP burn floors to zero. The if (exitBalances[i] == 0) continue; branch is thus not
  reachable by external price manipulation for a real position — it exists for genuinely reserve-empty/one-sided
  configurations.
  3. Independently, the entry point is wrong for the claimed threat model. __initiateWithdraw(account, ...) is reached
  only through the vault's withdraw flow for the account itself; an unprivileged third party cannot force someone else's
  position into a withdraw request. So even granting the impossible pool state, the "attacker" would be the position
  owner, converting their own collateral into an unredeemable state (the same one-legged request also makes
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest divide by w.sharesAmount == 0). That is uncompensated self-griefing — the actor loses
  their own equity and cannot extract the borrowed principal, which is not a profitable external exploit.
  4. What genuinely differs between hasPendingWithdrawals (which has if (address(manager) == address(0)) continue;) and
  getWithdrawRequestValue (which has no such skip and a bare require(hasRequest)) is an asymmetry over tokens with no
  registered manager — i.e. a registry/configuration condition set by a privileged role, not an unprivileged trigger.
  That is out of scope, and it is not the path the candidate actually asserts.

  Given the exit library is unshown, the pool-state precondition is unreachable by permissionless swapping under the
  invariant, and the trigger is owner-only self-harm rather than an external attack, the claim does not hold.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C15|A claim that a proportional multi-asset payout floors to zero must demonstrate that the required
  extreme reserve imbalance is actually reachable by permissionless trading against the pool's invariant — not merely
  asserted via "flash-swap" — and must identify a trigger callable by someone other than the position owner, since a
  state only the owner can enter at their own expense is griefing, not an external exploit.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/AbstractSingleSidedLP.sol:getWithdrawRequestValue|class=C15|The zero-leg
  precondition is unreachable: a proportional Curve exit only truncates to zero if a reserve is pushed to wei-scale,
  which the StableSwap invariant makes cost-divergent, and the withdraw request can in any case only be initiated by the
  position owner against their own collateral, so no unprivileged external attacker can strand a third party's
  position.
